WEBVTT

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:18.200
<v Ira Glass>This is Ira Glass, host of Your Radio Playhouse , and starting this week, WBEZ is moving the program that usually begins right at this time, Selected Shorts , to Friday nights at 8 o'clock. So if you want hear Selected Shorts tune in on Fridays at 8:00.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，是《你的广播剧场》的主持人。从本周开始，WBEZ电台将把通常在这个时间播出的节目《精选短篇》调整到每周五晚上8点播出。所以，如果您想收听《精选短篇》，请在周五晚上8点锁定频道。

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:49.140
<v Ira Glass>And WBEZ is rebroadcasting my Friday night program, Your Radio Playhouse , right here, right now after Prairie Home Companion . And there's a little radio programming experiment. The idea is that since my program is radio storytelling plus music, it has some things in common with Prairie Home Companion , and might be the kind of thing that Garrison Keillor fans would enjoy. WBEZ is going to try this out for a few months and WBEZ management welcomes your thoughts and reactions to this programming idea.
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ电台目前正在《草原家园伴侣》之后，重播我在周五晚上的节目《你的广播剧场》。这是一个小小的广播节目实验。我们的想法是，既然我的节目结合了广播故事和音乐，它与《草原家园伴侣》有共通之处，可能也是加里森·基勒的粉丝会喜欢的类型。WBEZ电台打算尝试几个月，管理层欢迎您对这一节目构想提出想法和反馈。

00:00:49.140 --> 00:01:04.300
<v Ira Glass>Your Radio Playhouse has been on the radio on Friday nights now for several months, and if you haven't heard it, the stories are told in a wide variety of styles by a wide variety of writers and performers. And some of the program just happens right here in the radio studio.
<v Ira Glass>《你的广播剧场》在周五晚上已经播出了好几个月了。如果您还没听过，节目中的故事是由各种各样的作家和表演者以多种风格讲述的。而且，节目中的一部分内容就在这个广播演播室里即兴发生。

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:39.100
<v Ira Glass>For example, this week's program is about love. It's for the week of Valentine's Day. And we begin with this cassette-- I have it here-- that a friend sent me for the Valentine's Day holiday. This is "A Guided Meditation For Singles" presented by Frayda Kafka. And normally, if you're going to make something like this up, you actually would give the person-- you'd make up a name like Frayda Kafka. But I'm afraid this all too real. Frayda Kafka, produced by something called Conscious Singles Connection Incorporated, copyright 1994.
<v Ira Glass>例如，本周的节目主题是爱情，正值情人节这一周。我们从这盘卡带开始——我把它带来了——这是一个朋友送给我的情人节礼物。这是由弗雷达·卡夫卡带来的《单身人士引导式冥想》。通常情况下，如果你要编造这种东西，你可能会给当事人取个像弗雷达·卡夫卡这样的名字。但我担心这实在是太真实了。弗雷达·卡夫卡，由“意识单身连接公司”制作，版权所有1994年。

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:50.100
<v Ira Glass>And this cassette has lots of ideas for things that singles-- conscious singles, presumably-- can do so they don't feel bad on holidays like Valentine's Day.
<v Ira Glass>这盘卡带里有很多点子，供单身人士——据推测是那些有意识的单身人士——在情人节这样的节日里可以做的事情，这样他们就不会感到难过。

00:01:50.100 --> 00:02:27.400
<v Frayda Kafka>You know, you can give yourself a very special day alone if that's what you're going to do with your holiday. You can prepare for it, actually prepare in advance. You can buy flowers for yourself. You can light candles. You can plan a special ritual, a special meal just for you. You can even write yourself letters about it, poems. You can listen to very wonderful music on that day.
<v Frayda Kafka>你知道，如果你打算一个人过节，你可以为自己安排一个非常特别的单身日。你可以为此做准备，真正地提前准备。你可以为自己买花，可以点蜡烛，可以为自己策划一个特别的仪式，一顿特别的餐点。你甚至可以为此给自己写信、写诗。那天你可以听非常美妙的音乐。

00:02:27.400 --> 00:03:08.340
<v Ira Glass>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's Your Radio Playhouse . I'm Ira Glass. And you know, you didn't have to prepare in advance for this hour. We have prepared the whole thing for you. We have set the table. We've lit the candles. It's all set. Oh, conscious singles. And couples, you couples who are sensitive to your consciously single friends. A very special hour for you, so stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《你的广播剧场》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。你知道吗，你们不需要为这一个小时提前做任何准备。我们已经为您准备好了一切。我们已经摆好了餐桌，点燃了蜡烛。一切就绪。哦，有意识的单身人士们。还有情侣们，你们这些对单身朋友保持敏感的情侣们。这是一个为您准备的特别节目，请继续收听。

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:35.000
<v Ira Glass>What's amazing about this tape to me is its utter lack of modesty about what it promises. It doesn't just promise to make you feel a little better about being single, it is categorical. It promises complete spiritual and emotional peace. When you're done listening to this cassette it says, "You will be at peace with being single. You will feel happy. You will feel fulfilled."
<v Ira Glass>这盘录音带让我感到惊奇的是，它对自己的承诺完全没有谦逊可言。它不仅仅承诺让你对单身感觉好一点，它是绝对的。它承诺给予完全的精神和情感上的平静。它说，当你听完这盘卡带，“你将与单身状态达成和解。你会感到快乐。你会感到满足。”

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.110
<v Frayda Kafka>And you know you can do it by simply relaxing.
<v Frayda Kafka>而且你知道，你只需要放松就能做到这一点。

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:43.190
<v Ira Glass>You know what could be easier? What could be easier?
<v Ira Glass>你知道还有什么更容易的吗？还有什么比这更容易的呢？

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:52.750
<v Frayda Kafka>All you need to do is pay attention to your breath.
<v Frayda Kafka>你所需要做的仅仅是关注你的呼吸。

00:03:52.750 --> 00:04:09.210
<v Ira Glass>This isn't-- I don't know, it's not working for me. Are you breathing there at home? Are you? If you're in your car, are you breathing right now? And is it happening, are you finding peace?
<v Ira Glass>这并不是——我不知道，这对我没用。你们在家里呼吸了吗？呼吸了吗？如果你在车里，你现在在呼吸吗？这有效果吗，你找到平静了吗？

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.690
<v Ira Glass>She also says this sentence, which I love.
<v Ira Glass>她还说了这样一句话，我很喜欢。

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.730
<v Frayda Kafka>And the rest is up to me.
<v Frayda Kafka>剩下的就由我来决定了。

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:36.620
<v Ira Glass>That is a sentence you never hear in the English language. Usually when people get into the construction and the rest is up to-- that's a pretty common construction in school, in sales situations, over the TV. You hear that a lot and people never go into the construction, and the rest is up to-- and end up with me. No, it's always you.
<v Ira Glass>这是你在英语中永远听不到的一句话。通常当人们用到“剩下的就由……”这个句式时——这在学校、销售场景或电视上是很常见的句式。你经常听到这个说法，但人们从来不会在“剩下的就由……”之后接上“我”。不，总是“你”。

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.950
<v Frayda Kafka>And the rest is up to me.
<v Frayda Kafka>剩下的就由我来决定了。

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:44.100
<v Ira Glass>She also says this other thing that I find mesmerizing, but incomprehensible. And it's this--
<v Ira Glass>她还说了另一件让我觉得着迷但又不可思议的事情。就是这个——

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.830
<v Frayda Kafka>You don't even need to listen.
<v Frayda Kafka>你甚至不需要去听。

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:55.240
<v Ira Glass>What is that about? You know, she's in the tape business. It's not like a video thing, it's a cassette with sound.
<v Ira Glass>这是什么意思？你知道，她是做磁带生意的。这又不是视频，这是一盘有声音的卡带。

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.860
<v Frayda Kafka>You don't even need to listen.
<v Frayda Kafka>你甚至不需要去听。

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:05.570
<v Ira Glass>If at the beginning of our radio show we would say to you, hi, we've been working really hard to prepare a really good show for you. And there's one other thing you've got to know--
<v Ira Glass>如果我们在广播节目开始时对你说：“嗨，我们一直在努力为您准备一个非常好的节目。还有一件事你必须知道——”

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.680
<v Frayda Kafka>You don't even need to listen.
<v Frayda Kafka>你甚至不需要去听。

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:18.940
<v Ira Glass>Unless it was some sort of reverse psychology thing. You know what I mean? We've used all the promotional tactics we could think of and now we're doing reverse psychology. We're doing reverse psychology and what we're saying is--
<v Ira Glass>除非这是一种逆向心理战。你明白我的意思吗？我们用尽了能想到的所有促销策略，现在我们在用逆向心理学。我们是在玩逆向心理学，我们想说的是——

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.470
<v Frayda Kafka>You don't even need to listen.
<v Frayda Kafka>你甚至不需要去听。

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:38.500
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, that will be the day. So side one of the tape is a kind of warm-up to the actual meditation, and side two is the guided meditation itself.
<v Ira Glass>是的，真有那天的话。所以磁带的第一面是实际冥想之前的热身，第二面是引导式冥想本身。

00:05:38.500 --> 00:06:02.180
<v Ira Glass>And she has us relax, breathe deeply, which we have all been practicing earlier in the show. And then she says, pretend you're having a dream. And the dream she has us pretend is astonishingly banal. You pretend we're floating among clouds.
<v Ira Glass>她让我们放松，深呼吸，这我们在节目早些时候都已经练习过了。然后她说，假装你在做梦。而她让我们假装做的那个梦简直平庸得惊人。你假装我们在云中漂浮。

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:21.580
<v Frayda Kafka>And you can let that cloud carry you, spiraling up over the roof. Floating over the trees. Way up over the mountains.
<v Frayda Kafka>你可以让那朵云带着你，盘旋在屋顶之上。漂浮在树林之上。高高地漂浮在群山之上。

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:30.620
<v Ira Glass>If you had to pay for dreams, this is one you wouldn't even rent. You wouldn't even rent this one at the video store.
<v Ira Glass>如果你必须为梦付费，这一个你甚至不会去租。你甚至不会在录像带店租这个梦。

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:53.460
<v Ira Glass>So you float in the dream, and of course because all the elements have to be involved, there's air and there's clouds, and you float passed the mountain. And then you come, of course, to a river. And this is where she gets to the heart of the matter. Remember the promise of the tape is that she is going to make you feel good about your singlehood, and at peace with it. And how does she do it?
<v Ira Glass>所以你在梦中漂浮，当然因为所有元素都必须参与其中，有空气，有云朵，你漂浮着经过了山脉。然后，当然，你来到了一条河流。这就是她切入问题核心的地方。记住，这盘卡带的承诺是让你对单身状态感觉良好，并与之和平共处。她是怎么做到的呢？

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:59.800
<v Ira Glass>It's really fascinating. She does it by having you imagine the person who you're meant to be with.
<v Ira Glass>真的很令人着迷。她让你想象那个你注定要在一起的人，从而实现这一点。

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:09.150
<v Frayda Kafka>This person understands you and loves you and knows exactly what to say to you.
<v Frayda Kafka>这个人理解你、爱你，并且确切地知道该对你说什么。

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:32.210
<v Ira Glass>In other words, the whole way that you're going to come to peace with your singlehood is you just envision the day when you're not single. That seems like kind of a cheat to me. That isn't being at peace with singlehood. That's simply believing that your singlehood is a temporarily delayed state that will lead to couplehood.
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，你与单身状态达成和解的全部方式，就是预想那个你不再单身的某一天。这在我看来有点像作弊。那不是与单身状态达成和解。那仅仅是相信你的单身状态是一个暂时延迟的状态，最终会走向双人状态。

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:39.770
<v Frayda Kafka>This person can be male or female. It may not even really be a person.
<v Frayda Kafka>这个人可以是男性，也可以是女性。甚至可能根本不是一个人。

00:07:39.770 --> 00:07:57.370
<v Ira Glass>What is that about? What is the bet that she's hedging there? Like, you know, who is she trying not to offend? Like, OK, we've got the people who are involved with the males. We've got the people involved with the females. And then, we just want to be sure like we get everybody in, the people who are involved with-- what, a horse?
<v Ira Glass>这是什么意思？她在打什么埋伏？就像，你知道，她试图不冒犯谁？比如，好吧，我们有和男性交往的人，我们有和女性交往的人。然后，我们只是想确保把每个人都包括进去，那些和——什么，马交往的人？

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:40.680
<v Frayda Kafka>And this person will come as close to you as you wish. And touch you in any way that feels good for you. Take your time now and let yourself feel the way you'd like to be touched. You might like just a touch on the shoulder, or an arm around you. You might like a hand on your forehead. Or maybe you'd like to be held or rocked. You can have whatever you want from this person.
<v Frayda Kafka>而这个人会尽可能地靠近你，以任何让你感觉良好的方式触碰你。现在花点时间，让自己感受一下你希望被怎样触碰。你可能只是喜欢肩膀上的触摸，或者被一只手臂环绕。你可能喜欢手放在额头上。或者你可能喜欢被拥抱或摇晃。你可以从这个人身上得到你想要的任何东西。

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:58.860
<v Ira Glass>At this point in the tape, I have to say-- for me, anyway-- it kind of starts to work on me. I start to think, like yes, I do want this person who's going to hold me and be the person who totally understands me.
<v Ira Glass>在磁带的这一点上，我必须说——至少对我来说——它开始对我起作用了。我开始想，是的，我确实想要这样一个人，他会拥抱我，成为那个完全理解我的人。

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:05.720
<v Frayda Kafka>This person is here for you from your future.
<v Frayda Kafka>这个人从你的未来来到这里。

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:28.800
<v Ira Glass>And one of the things that's interesting to me about this is not just that it's so effective, but that it's such a lie. I mean, if you've ever been in an actual relationship, no human being who you're going to be with is going to be there to touch you the way you want to be touched every time, and talk to you the way you want to be talked to every time.
<v Ira Glass>关于这一点，让我感兴趣的不仅是它如此有效，而且它是一个谎言。我的意思是，如果你曾经真正处于一段关系中，你所陪伴的任何一个人都不可能每次都以你想要的方式触碰你，也不可能每次都以你想要的方式跟你说话。

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:42.250
<v Ira Glass>And the fact that this could be a comfort to someone, I think it's just a sign of how much we want this. You know, that we want this person.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明这能给某人带来安慰，我认为这只是说明我们有多么渴望这一点。你知道，我们是多么渴望这个人。

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:58.330
<v Ira Glass>In the New Yorker magazine this week, John Updike writes about Lana Turner's seven husbands. He says, there's something ridiculous about a woman who take seven husbands. As if she had rummaged through the drawers of masculinity and come up with seven dwarfs.
<v Ira Glass>本周的《纽约客》杂志上，约翰·厄普代克写到了拉娜·特纳的七任丈夫。他说，一个结过七次婚的女人有些可笑。就好像她在男性的抽屉里翻找，结果找出了七个小矮人。

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:15.840
<v Ira Glass>And I think that actually, what's ridiculous about it isn't that there was anything wrong with those seven guys that they were dwarfs. What's ridiculous is the notion that she could be married to seven different people and be happy with none of them. That she, herself, would not be able to find happiness with any of those guys.
<v Ira Glass>我认为实际上，这可笑的地方不在于那七个人有什么问题导致他们成了小矮人。可笑的是那种认为她可以和七个不同的人结婚却一个都不快乐的想法。她自己无法与这些人中的任何一个找到幸福。

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:29.760
<v Ira Glass>And the reason why she didn't find happiness with those guys was cause she was waiting for another guy. She was waiting for the guy on this cassette who doesn't exist.
<v Ira Glass>她没有与那些人找到幸福的原因，是因为她在等待另一个人。她在等待这盘卡带上的那个根本不存在的男人。

00:10:29.760 --> 00:11:00.940
<v Ira Glass>You know, what do we do with that dream? The dream that love is going to knock at the door. Like in some melodrama. Like a TV soap opera. What do we do about it when we've heard love call before and watched ourselves mess it up? What do we do?
<v Ira Glass>你知道，我们该拿那个梦怎么办？那个爱情会敲门的梦。就像在某些情节剧中。就像电视肥皂剧一样。当我们曾经听到过爱的呼唤，却看着自己搞砸了它时，我们该怎么办？我们该怎么办？

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:10.700
<v Ira Glass>In this hour, that dream and the difficulty of that dream.
<v Ira Glass>在接下来的这一小时里，我们将探讨那个梦以及那个梦的困境。

00:11:10.700 --> 00:12:13.070
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC- "LOVE AND HAPPINESS" BY REV. AL GREEN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 阿尔·格林牧师的《爱与幸福》]

00:12:13.070 --> 00:13:08.890
<v Ira Glass>Well, our special pre-Valentine's Day love program in three acts. Act one, Yearning, with setbacks. Act two, Sex and Sex and Sex. Act three, A Wedding.
<v Ira Glass>那么，我们特别的情人节爱情节目分为三幕。第一幕：渴望，伴随着挫折。第二幕：性，性，还是性。第三幕：婚礼。

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.890
<v Ira Glass>Act one, Yearning.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：渴望。

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:42.730
<v Ira Glass>We begin with this image, a woman in a crushed red velvet dress, lying on her stomach on the ground. The dress has a train that's 60 feet long trailing behind her. Her face looks like the face of someone who's been making out in the backseat of a car for hours. Her mouth is swollen and puffy from kissing. Lipstick is smeared everywhere, on her mouth and chin. A dab is on her nose.
<v Ira Glass>我们以这个形象开始：一个穿着压碎的天鹅绒红裙的女人，肚子贴地躺在地上。裙子后面拖着一条60英尺长的裙摆。她的脸看起来像是在汽车后座缠绵了几个小时的人的脸。她的嘴因为亲吻而肿胀红肿。口红到处都是，嘴上、下巴上。鼻子上也沾了一点。

00:13:42.730 --> 00:14:00.790
<v Ira Glass>She's kissing the ground, methodically, with purpose. She kisses the ground, applies a fresh coat of lipstick, kisses again. Applies another color, kisses again. She's gone through 11 tubes of lipstick in 25 hours. There's that sweet lipsticky perfume in the air.
<v Ira Glass>她有条不紊地、有目的地亲吻着地面。她亲吻地面，补上一层新鲜的口红，再亲吻。换个颜色，再亲吻。在25个小时里，她用了11支口红。空气中弥漫着那种甜甜的口红味。

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:07.040
<v Ira Glass>She's spelling out words on the floor in lipstick. Each letter is two feet high.
<v Ira Glass>她用口红在地上拼写文字。每个字母有两英尺高。

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:20.320
<v Julie Laffin>The word's say, there was a time when I worshipped the ground you walked on.
<v Julie Laffin>文字写道：曾经有一段时间，我崇拜你走过的每一寸土地。

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:30.050
<v Julie Laffin>I'm making a monument to my ex-boyfriend.
<v Julie Laffin>我正在为我的前男友建立一座纪念碑。

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:47.250
<v Ira Glass>The woman's named Julie Laffin, and the ex-boyfriend she says, was everything she wanted to be-- creative, together. She said she did worship the ground he walked on. She said she was the kind of person who was always in love with the fantasy of being in love.
<v Ira Glass>这个女人名叫朱莉·拉芬，她说那个前男友曾是她想成为的一切——有创造力、为人稳重。她说她确实崇拜他走过的土地。她说她那种人总是沉溺于“沉溺于恋爱”的幻想中。

00:14:47.250 --> 00:15:00.380
<v Julie Laffin>And I've worked really hard to maintain that fantasy in the face of all information about reality, the reality of the situation.
<v Julie Laffin>为了维持那种幻想，我面对所有关于现实的信息——那种情况的现实，付出了巨大的努力。

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:21.560
<v Ira Glass>When she first started kissing the ground, in public, as performance art, a part of her wished that the ex-boyfriend would find out. Maybe he would see that she had put him behind her. Maybe he'd have regrets. Maybe he'd get in touch with her. Who among us has not known this particular mix of conflicting feelings towards an ex?
<v Ira Glass>当她第一次开始作为行为艺术在公共场合亲吻地面时，她内心一部分希望前男友能发现。也许他会看到她已经把他抛在脑后了。也许他会后悔。也许他会联系她。我们中间谁没有对前任有过这种复杂的矛盾情感呢？

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.600
<v Ira Glass>Julie Laffin moved onto other kissing projects.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·拉芬转向了其他的亲吻项目。

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:47.510
<v Julie Laffin>I kissed all the names of my ex-lovers onto public property. I kissed the names on parking meters and benches and sidewalks and glass windows. And when I kiss their names, I actually would flash to that person and really dwell on them. And it was this very kind of releasing activity.
<v Julie Laffin>我把所有前任的名字都亲吻在了公共设施上。我把名字印在停车计时器、长椅、人行道和玻璃窗上。当我亲吻这些名字时，我确实会闪回到那个人的身上，真正地沉浸在关于他们的回忆中。这是一种非常具有释放感的活动。

00:15:47.510 --> 00:16:13.960
<v Julie Laffin>When I got to the last person's name, I kissed it onto this big, concrete wall. And I thought, this is the last person in my life. This is the last relationship that I've had. This is actually the last relationship I will ever have. And I sort of saw myself as now I was going to take this role of the eternal spinster.
<v Julie Laffin>当我亲吻到最后一个人的名字时，我把它印在一堵巨大的水泥墙上。我想，这是我生命中的最后一个人。这是我拥有的最后一段关系。这实际上是我这辈子唯一一段最后的爱情。我把自己视为那个永恒的老处女。

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:33.720
<v Ira Glass>When she does these performance art pieces, kissing the ground, she says that sometimes she feels like this nonentity. People stand near her, talk about what she's doing as if she's not there. And when she leaves, they walk on the words she's made with her mouth, turn them into a big red blur on the ground.
<v Ira Glass>当她进行这些行为艺术表演，亲吻地面时，她说有时她感觉自己像个透明人。人们站在她附近，谈论她的行为，仿佛她不存在一样。当她离开时，他们踩在她用嘴唇写下的文字上，把它们变成地面上一团模糊的红色。

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:43.570
<v Ira Glass>And Julie Laffin says there's something about kissing, compulsively for hours, that's partly like being out of control and partly very peaceful. Meditative.
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·拉芬说，连续几个小时强迫性地亲吻，有一种既像是失控，又像是非常平静、冥想般的感觉。

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:50.930
<v Ira Glass>She slides along the ground, kissing out the pattern of each letter.
<v Ira Glass>她沿着地面滑动，亲吻出每个字母的图案。

00:16:50.930 --> 00:17:30.600
<v Julie Laffin>The other day, this guy came in selling peanuts for the homeless. And he walked in and he said, is this what I think it is? And I said, I don't know. What do you think it is? And he said, are these all lip marks? And I said, yes. And he said, I wish I were the guy that these were meant for.
<v Julie Laffin>前几天，有个家伙进来卖花生给无家可归者。他走进来问：“这是我想的那样吗？”我说：“我不知道。你觉得是什么？”他说：“这些全是唇印吗？”我说：“是的。”他说：“真希望我就是那个这些唇印为之而生的人。”

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:58.460
<v Ira Glass>There's a yearning for love that we feel when we've actually loved someone and been loved. And then there's the yearning for love that you feel if you have it. Mark  O'Brien is a writer in California, but because of a childhood case of polio, he lives most of each day in an iron lung on his back. He's the subject of this amazing little film, new film, called Breathing Lessons. And the film is remarkable because it's about a guy in an iron lung, but it is completely unsentimental.
<v Ira Glass>当我们真正爱过某人并被爱过时，会感受到一种对爱的渴望。如果你拥有爱，你会感受到另一种渴望。马克·奥布莱恩是加利福尼亚的一位作家，由于童年时患过小儿麻痹症，他每天大部分时间都躺在背后的铁肺里。他是一部名为《呼吸课》的令人惊叹的小型新纪录片的主角。这部片子非常了不起，因为它讲述的是一个生活在铁肺中的人，但却完全不感伤。

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:12.810
<v Ira Glass>And in certain moments, it's also pretty funny. The film is by Jessica Yu, and it was just at the Sundance Film Festival. And Jessica Yu gave us permission to excerpt a memorable little scene from the film here on our radio show.
<v Ira Glass>在某些时刻，它也非常有趣。这部电影由杰西卡·余执导，刚刚在圣丹斯电影节展出。杰西卡·余允许我们在我们的广播节目中节选电影中一个令人难忘的小片段。

00:18:12.810 --> 00:18:42.820
<v Ira Glass>Because he's in the iron lung, Mark O'Brien has attendants cook and help him out during the day. And during the film he explains that these days he always has men do this job. Because when he had women do the job, he kept falling in love with them. And the love was never reciprocated. He wrote about one of these women, "Her pale, perfect skin, her strong, fleshy legs drove me to ecstasies of despair. See, she'd talk with me as a human instead of her savagely crippled employer."
<v Ira Glass>因为他生活在铁肺里，马克·奥布莱恩白天需要护理人员做饭并帮助他。在影片中，他解释说，现在他总是雇佣男性来做这份工作。因为当他雇佣女性时，他总是会爱上她们，而这种爱从未得到回应。他曾写到过其中一位女性：“她苍白、完美的皮肤，强壮、丰满的双腿让我陷入了绝望的狂喜。你看，她和我谈话是把我当成一个人，而不是她那严重残疾的雇主。”

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:47.100
<v Ira Glass>Here's a little scene about desire and yearning from this film.
<v Ira Glass>这是影片中关于欲望和渴望的一小段场景。

00:18:47.100 --> 00:19:27.190
<v Mark O'Brien>I hired a sex surrogate in '87 or '86. I forget when. I felt very crazy. I was angry at all women for not falling in love with me because I'd fallen in love with several attendants and they all said it was a business relationship. A sex surrogate is a person who has some psychological training that works with their body, having sex with a client who is referred by a therapist.
<v Mark O'Brien>我在87年或86年雇佣了一位性代理人。我不记得是什么时候了。我感到非常疯狂。我生所有女人的气，因为她们没有爱上我，因为我爱上了好几个护理员，她们都说这只是生意关系。性代理人是一个经过心理训练，通过身体与客户进行性行为的人，这是由治疗师推荐的。

00:19:27.190 --> 00:19:46.760
<v Mark O'Brien>The surrogate had this big mirror and she showed me naked and aroused. I thought of myself as the ugliest man in the world. But it's like something, someone who would want to have sex with. Not just my dick, but my whole body.
<v Mark O'Brien>那位代理人有一面大镜子，她让我看到自己赤身裸体且产生性欲的样子。我曾认为自己是世界上最丑的男人。但这就像某种东西，有人愿意与之发生性关系的人。不仅仅是我的阴茎，而是我的整个身体。

00:19:46.760 --> 00:20:45.910
<v Mark O'Brien>And Carol was very kind to me. She kissed me on the chest after we had intercourse. I felt my chest was very unattractive, but she kissed me right there and the intercourse was so quick. I hate to say it, but it was wham, bam, thank you, ma'am. And it wasn't as great as I thought it would be, but being naked in a bed with a woman who's being extremely friendly was the most fun I've ever had. I think I'd like to do it again.
<v Mark O'Brien>卡萝尔对我非常友善。我们在性交后，她亲吻了我的胸部。我觉得我的胸部非常缺乏吸引力，但她就在那里亲吻了我，而且性交时间非常短。我不想说，但那真是“嘿咻一下，谢谢大姐”。虽然不像我想象的那样完美，但在床上赤身裸体地和一个极其友好的女人在一起，是我经历过最快乐的事。我想我愿意再做一次。

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:59.830
<v Ira Glass>Usually Mark O'Brien can't be outside of his iron lung for more than 45 minutes. But when the sex surrogate was with him, he was outside his tube for longer than that. Longer than he almost ever goes out. And he didn't even use his portable respirator.
<v Ira Glass>通常马克·奥布莱恩离开铁肺的时间不能超过45分钟。但当那位性代理人与他在一起时，他离开呼吸管的时间比那还要长。比他几乎任何时候出门的时间都长。而且他甚至没有使用便携式呼吸机。

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:16.790
<v Julie Laffin>I didn't need it for an hour. I went for an hour without it. They should think of sex as respiratory therapy. Maybe Medicare would pay for it.
<v Julie Laffin>我有一个小时不需要它。我离开它一个小时。他们应该把性视为呼吸治疗。也许医疗保险会为此买单。

00:21:16.790 --> 00:22:10.800
<v Julie Laffin>About a year after I last saw her, I just felt terribly depressed. I expected somehow that seeing the surrogate would change my life. I'd started wearing cologne and I thought everyone would be able to tell I was sexy and handsome. But nothing happened. They tell us to think of ourselves as sexual and beautiful, but it doesn't do any good unless someone else sees us as sexual and beautiful. You just can't demand love. You have to be lovable. I'm still trying to figure out how to do that.
<v Julie Laffin>在我最后一次见到她大约一年后，我感到非常沮丧。我原本期待见到那位代理人会改变我的生活。我开始喷古龙水，以为每个人都能看出来我很性感、很英俊。但什么也没发生。他们告诉我们要认为自己是性感和美丽的，但除非别人也这样看你，否则毫无用处。你不能强求爱。你必须是一个值得爱的人。我还在努力弄明白怎么做到这一点。

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.530
<v Luis Rodriguez>This is Luis Rodriquez and the piece is called "Waiting."
<v Luis Rodriguez>这是路易斯·罗德里格斯的作品，题目是《等待》。

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:22.020
<v Luis Rodriguez>What made the waiting so painful? The woman had called, she was on her way.
<v Luis Rodriguez>是什么让等待变得如此痛苦？那个女人打过电话，说她在路上了。

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:34.720
<v Luis Rodriguez>Tense, I waited sitting at the kitchen table. There had been too many nights waking up to bottles and books on the floor. Two small children parked on blankets in the corner.
<v Luis Rodriguez>我紧张地坐在餐桌旁等待。有过太多次在瓶子和书本散落一地的地板上醒来的夜晚。两个小孩在角落里的毯子上睡着。

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:42.240
<v Luis Rodriguez>Alone ain't so bad. Dreams of women yet to be touched, to be smelled. It ain't bad.
<v Luis Rodriguez>独自一人也并不坏。梦想着尚未被触碰、尚未被闻到的女性。那并不坏。

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:53.700
<v Luis Rodriguez>Up on a hill hidden by wood and shingled shacks alongside curbless roads visited by nobody unless they had to be here. Alone ain't so bad.
<v Luis Rodriguez>在山上，隐藏在木头和木瓦小屋旁，沿着没人光顾的没有路缘的道路。独自一人也并不坏。

00:22:53.700 --> 00:23:10.620
<v Luis Rodriguez>Hammering holes into walls with fists, tears streaming down my face at saxophone riffs. Looking at old photos, feeding babies, taking out trash, and thinking of her. It ain't so bad. I couldn't stand it.
<v Luis Rodriguez>用拳头在墙上砸出洞，听着萨克斯的乐句眼泪直流。看着旧照片，喂孩子，倒垃圾，想着她。那并不坏。我简直无法忍受。

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:30.300
<v Luis Rodriguez>Looked through personal ads in the weeklys. Made phone calls, wrote letters, came across a video date, the godsend for lonely people. I called. After three false starts, a lady said she would be here Saturday. It's Saturday and I'm going nuts.
<v Luis Rodriguez>翻看周刊上的个人征友广告。打电话，写信，碰到了视频约会，这是给孤独者的天赐之物。我打了电话。经过三次失败的尝试，一位女士说她周六会来。现在是周六，我要疯了。

00:23:30.300 --> 00:24:01.910
<v Luis Rodriguez>An hour finding the place, an old stucco white basement room. You can't miss it. Cholo graffiti on the front door. She came, made her pits. Saw the hurt in my eyes. I always spoke with my eyes, dammit. A longing for sweet companionship. Not of drunken home boys or angel dusted street women, for the mother of my kids out at Sunny's Lounge sucking Kahluas and highballs, and shooting out wicked smiles at discoed-out dudes.
<v Luis Rodriguez>花了一个小时才找到那个地方，一个老式的灰泥白色地下室房间。你不会错过的。前门上有涂鸦。她来了，摆出姿势。看到了我眼里的伤痛。我总是用眼睛说话，该死。一种对甜蜜陪伴的渴望。不是醉醺醺的狐朋狗友，也不是嗑药的街头女人，而是我孩子们的母亲，在桑尼酒廊喝着卡鲁瓦咖啡甜酒和烈酒，对着那些迪斯科打扮的男人露出邪恶的微笑。

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:15.940
<v Luis Rodriguez>The lady looked in my eyes and then stopped. Refused to sell me the video date. Refused to take the check. This can't help you, she said, and walked out.
<v Luis Rodriguez>那位女士看着我的眼睛，然后停了下来。拒绝卖给我视频约会。拒绝收那张支票。“这帮不了你，”她说，然后走了出去。

00:24:15.940 --> 00:24:22.430
<v Dolores Wilbur>He asked me to go for a walk. I said no, even though I knew I wanted to.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他让我去散步。我说不，尽管我知道我内心想去。

00:24:22.430 --> 00:24:25.770
<v Ira Glass>This story about wanting love from Dolores Wilbur.
<v Ira Glass>这个关于渴望爱的故事来自多洛莉丝·威尔伯。

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:34.670
<v Dolores Wilbur>We were sweeping out the cottage, cleaning up. It was time to go home. We had to leave that afternoon, less than a couple of hours away.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我们在打扫小屋，清理东西。该回家了。那天下午我们要离开，不到几个小时就要走。

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:44.680
<v Dolores Wilbur>He just looked at me without expression, not sure of how he should look. He knew I would go for a walk. He knew I wanted to.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他只是面无表情地看着我，不确定该表现出什么神情。他知道我会去散步。他知道我想去。

00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:59.430
<v Dolores Wilbur>I knew I would as well, but I wanted to say no to try it out. I gave in almost immediately. So we walked. I kicked stones watching my feet. There were trees on either side of us.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我也知道我会去的，但我还是想说不，试探一下。我几乎立刻就妥协了。于是我们散步。我踢着石头，看着自己的脚。我们两侧都是树。

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:05.540
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, I don't have anything to say. I just wanted to walk with you.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，我没什么可说的。我只是想和你走走。

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:14.230
<v Dolores Wilbur>I said stupid things. Or maybe it is that they weren't stupid, but saying them out loud sounded so stupid.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我说了一些愚蠢的话。或者也许不是愚蠢的话，但大声说出来听起来太蠢了。

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.340
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, how do you feel?
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，你感觉怎么样？

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:29.840
<v Dolores Wilbur>I said, upset. He  nodded. I hated that he nodded. He knew it was going badly and he began to feel miserable. He tried to think of something to say.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我说，很难受。他点了点头。我讨厌他点头。他知道事情进展得很糟，他开始感到难过。他努力想说些什么。

00:25:29.840 --> 00:25:47.560
<v Dolores Wilbur>I said, I don't understand you. We thought we were alike, but we find we are not. I hate when you say you're sorry you hurt my feelings. You didn't hurt my feelings, you hurt me. And you're dumb. Why are you doing this? I hate this conversation. I hate this kind of conversation.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我说，我不理解你。我们以为我们很像，但发现我们并不像。我讨厌你说你很抱歉伤害了我的感情。你没有伤害我的感情，你伤害了我。你真笨。你为什么要这么做？我讨厌这场对话。我讨厌这种对话。

00:25:47.560 --> 00:25:52.190
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, I hate this conversation too. I hate it so much.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，我也讨厌这场对话。我太讨厌它了。

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:56.740
<v Dolores Wilbur>I said, well, you're the man. You're the one making it all wrong. You figure it out.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我说，好吧，你是男人。是你把一切都搞错了。你自己去解决吧。

00:25:56.740 --> 00:26:01.270
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, I'm the one who's supposed to fix it, is that it?
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，我是那个应该去解决它的人，是这样吗？

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:06.140
<v Dolores Wilbur>I said, we can't be friends. We can't. I can't. It's not what I want.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我说，我们不能做朋友。我们不能。我做不到。这不是我想要的。

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:12.710
<v Dolores Wilbur>He nodded, looked down, looked away. Looked anxious, afraid to say anything.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他点了点头，低下头，移开目光。看起来很焦虑，害怕说什么。

00:26:12.710 --> 00:26:19.380
<v Dolores Wilbur>After a while he said, how you feel now, this has happened to me before.
<v Dolores Wilbur>过了一会儿，他说，你现在感觉如何，这种事以前也发生在我身上。

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.990
<v Dolores Wilbur>What? I said.
<v Dolores Wilbur>什么？我说。

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:33.720
<v Dolores Wilbur>He told me something that only made me mad. He wanted someone who didn't want him and it took a long time to be over. So what, I thought. Who cares? It's not the same.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他告诉我的一些事只会让我更生气。他曾经想要一个不想要他的人，那花了很长时间才走出来。那又怎样，我想。谁在乎呢？这不一样。

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:36.530
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, can't we be friends?
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，我们不能做朋友吗？

00:26:36.530 --> 00:26:40.350
<v Dolores Wilbur>We can talk on the phone, but we can't see each other, I said.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我们可以通电话，但不能见面，我说。

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:43.250
<v Dolores Wilbur>But will we talk to be friends? He said.
<v Dolores Wilbur>但我们要为了做朋友而交流吗？他说。

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:46.060
<v Dolores Wilbur>No, I said.
<v Dolores Wilbur>不，我说。

00:26:46.060 --> 00:26:50.620
<v Dolores Wilbur>He said, what will we say? Hi, how are you? I'm fine. That's all?
<v Dolores Wilbur>他说，我们要说什么？嘿，你好吗？我很好。就这样吗？

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:53.690
<v Dolores Wilbur>I don't know, I said.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我不知道，我说。

00:26:53.690 --> 00:27:17.730
<v Dolores Wilbur>We walked aimlessly. It was hot. We looked for shade. He stopped at a bunch of rocks marking the gate in front of a white picket fence. He picked up one of the rocks with both hands. It was big and rough, salt and pepper with white spindly lines running through it. Some bits of sparkly dust glinted off it in the sun.
<v Dolores Wilbur>我们漫无目的地走着。天气很热。我们寻找阴凉处。他在一堆石头旁停了下来，那是白色尖头栅栏前的门标。他用双手捡起其中一块石头。又大又粗糙，布满了盐和胡椒般的白色细纹。太阳下闪烁着些许火花。

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:26.570
<v Dolores Wilbur>He stood holding it to his chest for a moment and then, looking around, gently put it down somewhere else, less than a foot away.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他把它贴在胸口站了一会儿，然后环顾四周，轻轻地把它放到了不到一英尺远的别处。

00:27:26.570 --> 00:27:49.200
<v Dolores Wilbur>There, doesn't it look better now? He said. He looked satisfied, relieved at what he had done. I fought back irritation and wondered at what this meant to him. I guessed it was because he had done something. Sometimes just doing any one thing can make you feel better. It doesn't matter what.
<v Dolores Wilbur>瞧，现在看起来是不是好多了？他说。他看起来很满意，对自己所做的事情感到如释重负。我克制住心中的烦躁，想知道这对意味着什么。我猜是因为他做了某件事。有时仅仅做某事就能让你感觉好一点。是什么并不重要。

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:56.020
<v Dolores Wilbur>The look on his face picking up the rock, moving it, walking, it all made me so tired.
<v Dolores Wilbur>他捡起石头、移动它、走路时脸上的表情，这一切都让我感到无比疲惫。

