00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:43.110
<v Nancy Updike>But then fear faded to guilt, and now I don't even think the guilt makes sense. I'm not using condoms and my women friends are not using condoms because when we look around, we are not dying of this disease, and neither is anyone we're sleeping with. And it's been that way for 10 years now. Heterosexual, non-drug-using women simply have not, as a group, become the much-feared second wave of the AIDS epidemic as we were always being warned in college that we would.
<v Nancy Updike>但随后恐惧变成了内疚，现在我甚至觉得内疚都没有意义了。我不用避孕套，我的女性朋友们也不用，因为当我们环顾四周时，我们并没有死于这种疾病，我们发生关系的任何人也没有。这种情况已经持续10年了。异性恋、不吸毒的女性作为一个群体，并没有像我们在大学里一直被警告的那样，成为令人恐惧的艾滋病疫情的第二波爆发。

00:44:43.110 --> 00:45:13.420
<v Nancy Updike>Only 8% of all diagnosed AIDS cases in the United States are from heterosexual contact, according to the CDC. But we keep calling ourselves lazy, my friends and I, I think because we're feeling like bad feminists. Giving up safe sex means giving up that solidarity we believed in back when it seemed like we were all going to be at risk together-- gay men, straight women, women across lines of race, and class, and age, all of us united against a single, deadly common enemy.
<v Nancy Updike>根据美国疾控中心的数据，美国所有诊断出的艾滋病病例中，只有8%是通过异性恋传播的。但我们还是管自己叫“懒惰”，我觉得那是因为我们觉得自己是糟糕的女性主义者。放弃安全性行为意味着放弃我们在相信我们要共同承担风险时所信仰的那种团结——男同性恋者、异性恋女性，跨越种族、阶级和年龄的女性，我们所有人团结一致对抗一个单一的、致命的共同敌人。

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:24.270
<v Nancy Updike>Five years later, I think it's only a matter of time before the phrase, "No glove, no love" sounds to us the way "Sisterhood is powerful" sounds to our mothers.
<v Nancy Updike>五年后，我想，“没有套就没有爱”这句话听起来像“姐妹力量大”听起来像我们母亲那辈的事，只是时间问题而已。

00:45:24.270 --> 00:47:33.590
<v Nancy Updike>[MUSIC- "FORBIDDEN LOVE" BY MADONNA]
<v Nancy Updike>[音乐 - 麦当娜的《禁忌之爱》]

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:37.210
<v Ira Glass>Act three, A Wedding.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕：婚礼。

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:48.360
<v Ira Glass>Shakespeare ends his comedies with weddings, and it's easy to see why. You want a moment of joy, of hope at the end of any program that touches on the idea of love.
<v Ira Glass>莎士比亚以婚礼结束他的喜剧，原因很容易理解。在任何触及爱情观念的节目结尾，你都需要一个充满喜悦和希望的时刻。

00:47:48.360 --> 00:47:58.260
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>OK, hold his hand. All right, now whatever I say in English, you're going to tell him in Spanish.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>好吧，握住他的手。好的，我现在用英语说的任何话，你都要用西班牙语告诉他。

00:47:58.260 --> 00:48:21.190
<v Ira Glass>If you walk downstairs in Chicago's City Hall, you take the escalator down when you walk in that front door on whatever street that is. If you walk downstairs and you turn right, you come to marriage court. It costs $10. There's a big sign saying right up front you can't offer the judge a gratuity. And you know, you get the license down the hall and then there's a one-day waiting period, like if you buy a handgun or something. They don't want people rushing into it.
<v Ira Glass>如果你走进芝加哥市政厅，从那条街的前门走进去，你会坐自动扶梯下楼。如果你下楼向右转，你就到了婚姻法庭。费用是10美元。门口有个大牌子写着你不能给法官小费。你知道，你在走廊领证，然后有一个为期一天的等待期，就像你买手枪一样。他们不希望人们操之过急。

00:48:21.190 --> 00:48:28.920
<v Ira Glass>12,530 people were married in this court last year. That's more than any other place in Illinois, as best as anybody can figure.
<v Ira Glass>去年有12530人在这个法庭结婚。据任何人估计，这比伊利诺伊州的任何其他地方都要多。

00:48:28.920 --> 00:48:46.280
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>I'm going to ask them about eight questions. Now, I don't want them to answer each question. When I'm all done with the eight questions-- ocho-- then I'll say to him, OK, Francisco, what is your answer? And he'll answer once. Tell him that.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>我会问他们大约八个问题。我不希望他们回答每个问题。等我问完八个问题（ocho）后，我会对他说：“好的，弗朗西斯科，你的回答是什么？”然后他回答一次。告诉他这一点。

00:48:46.280 --> 00:49:17.950
<v Ira Glass>Wednesday morning Francisco and Arminia Orosco stood before Judge Arthur Rosenblum. Arminia spoke English, but Francisco didn't. And although is was happening in a basement office with dingy carpet, and although the groom was in jeans with a Coors belt buckle and a leather jacket, and although they brought no witnesses, and although both of them were planning on going to work after the wedding-- her at an insurance office and him at a record store-- there was still something moving about watching her translate the vows, looking in the eyes of her husband-to-be. That is, once they actually got started.
<v Ira Glass>周三上午，弗朗西斯科和阿米尼亚·奥罗斯科站在亚瑟·罗森布鲁姆法官面前。阿米尼亚说英语，但弗朗西斯科不说。尽管这一切发生在一个铺着破旧地毯的地下室办公室里，尽管新郎穿着牛仔裤、带着库尔斯皮带扣和皮夹克，尽管他们没带证人，尽管他们两人都计划在婚礼后去上班——她在保险公司，他在唱片店——看着她翻译誓言，凝视着未婚夫眼睛的样子，依然有一些动人的东西。也就是说，一旦他们真正开始的话。

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:20.980
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>OK, ready? Here we go.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>好的，准备好了吗？我们开始吧。

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:33.060
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Francisco, do you take her to be your wife, and to live together with her as husband and wife? What are you looking at me for? Translate it.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>弗朗西斯科，你愿意娶她为妻，并作为夫妻共同生活吗？你看着我干什么？翻译啊。

00:49:33.060 --> 00:49:37.830
<v Arminia>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Arminia>[说西班牙语]

00:49:37.830 --> 00:49:40.890
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>And live together with her as husband and wife.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>并作为夫妻共同生活。

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:42.780
<v Arminia>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Arminia>[说西班牙语]

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:47.580
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Do you promise to love her, honor her, and respect her?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>你承诺爱她、尊重她并敬重她吗？

00:49:47.580 --> 00:49:50.040
<v Arminia>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Arminia>[说西班牙语]

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:52.830
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Do you promise to be her friend?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>你承诺做她的朋友吗？

00:49:52.830 --> 00:49:54.080
<v Arminia>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Arminia>[说西班牙语]

00:49:54.080 --> 00:50:00.770
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Do you promise to take care of her whether she's sick or whether she's well?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>你承诺照顾她，无论生病还是健康吗？

00:50:00.770 --> 00:50:03.760
<v Arminia>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Arminia>[说西班牙语]

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:18.500
<v Ira Glass>Judge Rosenblum looks in their eyes as he does the ceremony. He doesn't have a piece of paper or anything with the script. And after both of them did their vows, Judge Rosenblum gave a little speech about the wedding rings, about how they symbolize an endless love, an endless friendship. And both of them put on the rings.
<v Ira Glass>罗森布鲁姆法官在举行仪式时会凝视他们的眼睛。他没有拿着脚本纸或类似的东西。在他们两人宣读誓言后，罗森布鲁姆法官发表了一段关于结婚戒指的小演讲，谈到它们如何象征着无尽的爱和无尽的友谊。他们两人都戴上了戒指。

00:50:18.500 --> 00:50:23.490
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>All right, you're Senor Esposa. [SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>好的，现在你们是夫妻了（Senor Esposa）。[说西班牙语]

00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:25.350
<v Ira Glass>A big embrace, he says.
<v Ira Glass>他说，拥抱一下。

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:26.860
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Beso, beso.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>吻一下，吻一下。

00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:30.580
<v Ira Glass>Kiss, kiss. So they do.
<v Ira Glass>亲吻，亲吻。于是他们照做了。

00:50:30.580 --> 00:50:38.240
<v Ira Glass>Judge Rosenblum has done a few things to make weddings in city hall a little more human. He's replaced the generic hotel art in the office with a Norman Rockwell print.
<v Ira Glass>罗森布鲁姆法官做了一些事情，让市政厅的婚礼更具人性化。他把办公室里通用的酒店艺术品换成了诺曼·洛克威尔的版画。

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:52.350
<v Ira Glass>One whole wall of his office is lined with snapshots of happy couples, him in the middle. In this office, you see the whole range of couples, all ages and races, all incomes. Though a lot of people come to City Hall because it's so cheap. And the full range of happy and unhappy couples.
<v Ira Glass>他办公室的一整面墙上都贴满了幸福夫妇的照片，他就在中间。在这个办公室里，你看到了各种各样的夫妻，所有年龄和种族，所有收入水平。虽然很多人因为便宜才来市政厅。这里还有幸福和不幸福夫妇的全部范围。

00:50:52.350 --> 00:51:05.180
<v Ira Glass>Joan Healey is the [UNINTELLIGIBLE] good humor, completely wonderful woman who greets you when you walk into the office and arrive at the front window. She's the one who takes your $10. And she says that she sees some couples she frankly has to worry about a little.
<v Ira Glass>琼·希利是一位在市政厅前台接待你、非常有幽默感、绝对了不起的女士。就是她收你的10美元。她说，她看到有些夫妇，坦率地说，她不得不有点担心。

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:12.290
<v Joan>I really think, walk away. Walk away. It's a long life together, long life together.
<v Joan>我真的在想，离开吧。离开吧。生活在一起很漫长，漫长的生活。

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:15.770
<v Ira Glass>And what are you seeing when you think that? What are they doing?
<v Ira Glass>当你这么想的时候，你看到了什么？他们在做什么？

00:51:15.770 --> 00:51:51.470
<v Joan>I get a funny feeling in the tone of voice that they use to one another. This is kidding on the square, you are seeing this. And you're kind of hurting that person. Or just maybe sitting in a chair. If one will sit, go and sit in the chair, and the one that I kind of think is not the nice one would probably go sit in another chair on the other side of the room. Not really even wanting to be together, things like that. Just kind of shake your head and say-- by the time you get up to the escalator, the top of the escalator, I hope they're still together.
<v Joan>我从他们对彼此说话的语气中有一种奇怪的感觉。这种“开玩笑”背后其实是认真的，你看到了。你觉得你在伤害那个人。或者只是可能坐在椅子上。如果一个人坐下，去另一张椅子坐，我心想那个人可能不是那个好人，大概会去房间另一边坐另一张椅子。不想在一起，诸如此类的。只是摇摇头说——等到你上了电梯，上了自动扶梯顶端，我希望他们还能在一起。

00:51:51.470 --> 00:52:13.100
<v Ira Glass>In his chambers, between weddings, Judge Rosenblum reads and smokes a pipe. He was out til midnight the night before I visited in his regular gin rummy game. He's 79, married twice, but not currently married. He says that there's nothing the rabbi could have said to him under the marriage canopy when he got married that would've been useful advice. Each marriage runs the course it's supposed to, he says. It's just dumb luck. Nobody can really tell you anything.
<v Ira Glass>在法官办公室里，婚礼间隙，罗森布鲁姆法官会看书抽烟斗。我拜访他前一晚，他直到午夜才结束他的常规金拉米牌局。他79岁，结过两次婚，目前未婚。他说，当他结婚时，拉比在婚礼下对他说的任何话都不会是有用的建议。每一段婚姻都有它注定的历程，他说。这纯粹是运气。没人能真正告诉你什么。

00:52:13.100 --> 00:52:25.690
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Some should marry, some should not. Some do, some will last. Some won't last. That's true of all life in anything, right?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>有些人应该结婚，有些人不应该。有些人会结，有些人能持久。有些不会持久。这适用于生活中的任何事，对吧？

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:31.800
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Joan, let me ask you something? Why do people get married?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>琼，让我问你点事？为什么人们要结婚？

00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:38.660
<v Joan>Oh, for various reasons. Some for love, some for money. Some I don't know why.
<v Joan>哦，原因各异。有些为了爱，有些为了钱。有些我不知道为什么。

00:52:38.660 --> 00:52:39.950
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>Well, you married for money, right?
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>好吧，你是为了钱才结婚的，对吧？

00:52:39.950 --> 00:52:43.460
<v Joan>Oh, yes. Can't you tell?
<v Joan>哦，是的。你看不出来吗？

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:55.880
<v Ira Glass>If couples seem interested, Judge Rosenblum will pull a book out of his upper left-hand desk drawer and read a little bit. The book is Kahlil Gibran's The Prophet.
<v Ira Glass>如果夫妇看起来很感兴趣，罗森布鲁姆法官会从办公桌左上角的抽屉里拿出一本书读一小段。那本书是卡里·纪伯伦的《先知》。

00:52:55.880 --> 00:53:09.390
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>And this book is a great allegory. An allegory is a story like a parable. And what it does is it tells you something which really means something else.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>这本书是一则伟大的寓言。寓言就像一个比喻故事。它的作用是告诉你一些真正意味着其他含义的东西。

00:53:09.390 --> 00:53:12.050
<v Ira Glass>He turns to the chapters on love and marriage.
<v Ira Glass>他翻到关于爱和婚姻的章节。

00:53:12.050 --> 00:53:17.960
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>I'll only read the parts that are worldwide known.
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>我只读那些广为人知的部分。

00:53:17.960 --> 00:53:38.830
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>But if in your fear you would seek only love's peace and love's pleasure, then it is better for you that you cover your nakedness and pass out of love's threshing floor. In the seasonless world where you shall laugh, but not with all of your laughter, and weep, but not with all of your tears."
<v Judge Arthur Rosenblum>“但如果你们因恐惧而只想寻求爱的平静和爱的快乐，那么最好掩盖你们的赤裸，离开爱的脱粒场。走向那个无季节的世界，在那里你们可以欢笑，但不是尽情欢笑；可以哭泣，但不是尽情哭泣。”

00:53:38.830 --> 00:53:47.570
<v Ira Glass>It can take away your faith in the romance of marriage to see 12,000 of them in a year. But Judge Rosenblum says he sees his share of truly happy couples in this job.
<v Ira Glass>看到一年里发生12000次婚姻，可能会削弱你对婚姻浪漫的信仰。但罗森布鲁姆法官说，他在这份工作中看到了属于他的那份真正幸福的夫妇。

00:53:47.570 --> 00:53:54.130
<v Ira Glass>The other woman working up front, Michelle Roberts, says that despite this job, she's still glad to be married when she goes home at night.
<v Ira Glass>前台工作的另一位女士米歇尔·罗伯茨说，尽管从事这份工作，她晚上回家时仍然很高兴自己已经结婚了。

00:53:54.130 --> 00:54:01.590
<v Ira Glass>And every day, dozens more arrive to take their vows. Some dressed in formal wear, some in jeans. Many with children in tow.
<v Ira Glass>每天都有几十对新人赶来宣读誓言。有的穿着礼服，有的穿着牛仔裤。许多人带着孩子。

00:54:01.590 --> 00:54:29.300
<v Ira Glass>Valentine's Day turns out to be the busiest day of the year here. 145 marriages last Valentine's Day. The halls were jammed. The elevator jammed. The escalator jammed with people who couldn't wait to get married on the holiday of love.
<v Ira Glass>情人节成了这里一年中最忙的一天。去年的情人节有145场婚礼。大厅拥挤不堪。电梯堵塞。自动扶梯上挤满了迫不及待想在爱的节日结婚的人们。

00:54:29.300 --> 00:57:15.900
<v Ira Glass>Well, before we end our show, there's a song that we've actually been saving to play for Valentine's Day. We recorded this back around Christmas. And these are kids at the Daniel J. Nellum Youth Services in Chicago. And they sing this little song that they made up, a little crush song. They include Antwone Robinson, Warren Harris, Sean Cook, Edward G. Robinson, Junior, Willie Weddington, Curtis Perry, and Nate Settles. Here's their song.
<v Ira Glass>好了，在结束节目之前，有一首歌我们实际上一直留着要在情人节播放。我们是在圣诞节前后录制的。这是芝加哥丹尼尔·J·内勒姆青少年服务中心的孩子们。他们唱了这首他们自创的小歌，一首关于暗恋的歌。他们包括安特旺·鲁滨逊、沃伦·哈里斯、肖恩·库克、爱德华·G·鲁滨逊二世、威利·韦丁顿、柯蒂斯·佩里和内特·塞特尔斯。这是他们的歌。

00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:24.890
<v Ira Glass>Tonight's program was produced by Dolores Wilbur and by myself, with Alix Spiegel, Peter Clowney and Nancy Updike. Contributing editors Jack Hitt, Margy Rockland and Mr. Paul Tough.
<v Ira Glass>今晚的节目由多洛莉丝·威尔伯和我本人制作，艾莉克斯·斯皮格尔、彼得·克洛尼和南希·厄普代克参与制作。特约编辑杰克·希特、玛吉·洛克兰和保罗·塔夫先生。

00:57:24.890 --> 00:57:33.960
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia. That's a really good name to mess up during the credits.
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责。在片尾字幕中弄错这个名字真是太棒了。

00:57:33.960 --> 00:58:16.360
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:26.560
<v Ira Glass>If you would care to buy a tape of this or our other Radio Playhouse programs, call us. Call us, call us at WBEZ 312-832-3380.
<v Ira Glass>如果您想购买本节目或其他广播剧场节目的录音带，请致电我们。致电我们，拨打WBEZ电台 312-832-3380。

00:58:26.560 --> 00:58:33.810
<v Ira Glass>You can email us with any comments or thoughts and we will email you back. Our address, [email protected] .
<v Ira Glass>您可以给我们发送电子邮件，提出任何意见或想法，我们将回复您。我们的地址是 [email protected]。

00:58:33.810 --> 00:58:46.090
<v Ira Glass>And do you remember that really smart, amazing guy in the iron lung? You can actually email him. His name is Mark O'Brien if you want to chat with him. And his email address is marko-- M-A-R-K-O-- @well.com.
<v Ira Glass>还记得那个生活在铁肺里的极其聪明、了不起的人吗？您可以给他发电子邮件。他的名字叫马克·奥布莱恩，如果您想和他聊天的话。他的电子邮件地址是 marko-- M-A-R-K-O-- @well.com。

00:58:46.090 --> 00:58:51.090
<v Ira Glass>We, this Radio Playhouse program, we broadcast proudly from WBEZ Chicago. We'll be back next week with more stories of this here American life, I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>我们，这个广播剧场节目，自豪地从芝加哥WBEZ电台播出。下周我们将带回更多关于美国生活的故事，我是艾拉·格拉斯。
